  {title:"Two Patterns Together",explanation:"Consonant-stem nouns (lēx, frāter, pater, māter, clāmor, prīnceps) form the genitive plural in -um; i-stem nouns (pars, mōns, collis, hostis, gēns, caedēs) form it in -ium.",exampleLatin:"frāter, frātris",exampleEnglish:"brother, of a brother",tip:"The nominative often loses letters — frāter sheds letters to become frātris."},
  {title:"Lost Letters",explanation:"The nominative singular often loses letters: frāter, frātris (brother) and mōns, montis (mountain). The genitive shows the real stem.",exampleLatin:"clāmōrem audīvit",exampleEnglish:"he heard the shout",tip:"mōns ends in -s after a consonant, so it is an i-stem: montium."}
